1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,000
[01] Wake up on the mud roof beside the Nile

2
00:00:07,000 --> 00:00:13,000
[02] No coffee, no weekend, and a man with a blank scroll

3
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:22,000
[03] Your grain, and roughly 14 hours to survive

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:28,000
[04] Not a pharaoh, not a priest, statistically a farmer

5
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:34,000
[05] A mud brick house in a village you'll never leave

6
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:40,000
[06] 3000 years of history, so we pick the New Kingdom

7
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:46,000
[07] The stretch with the most everyday evidence: houses, receipts

8
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:54,000
[08] Sick notes and arguments. Right, get up

9
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:01,000
[09] Nile mud mixed with straw, dried in the sun

10
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:07,000
[10] Thick walls, tiny high windows, cool inside

11
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:13,000
[11] The roof is kitchen, storage and spare bedroom

12
00:01:13,000 --> 00:01:19,000
[12] Shared with too many relatives, and mice

13
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:26,000
[13] Linen from flax: a kilt and a straight dress

14
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:33,000
[14] Children wore nothing; nearly everyone wore eye paint

15
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:40,000
[15] Ground galena that cut the glare like an athlete

16
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,000
[16] Two lead compounds that don't occur naturally in Egypt

17
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:52,000
[17] Somebody was manufacturing them deliberately, over weeks

18
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:58,000
[18] A lab study: an immune signal boosted in cultured cells

19
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:07,000
[19] Bread and beer for breakfast, lunch and dinner

20
00:02:07,000 --> 00:02:14,000
[20] Beer thick and filling, closer to porridge

21
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:23,000
[21] Onions, dates, cucumbers, fish, and festival meat

22
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
[22] Grain ground on stone, grit shed into the flour

23
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:36,000
[23] Skulls with teeth worn flat and abscesses in the jaw

24
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:42,000
[24] And there were no dentists

25
00:02:42,000 --> 00:02:47,000
[25] Once a year the Nile floods and drops black silt

26
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:54,000
[26] The Black Land, named after the mud not the sand

27
00:02:54,000 --> 00:03:01,000
[27] Three seasons: the flood, the growing, the harvest

28
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:06,000
[28] Fields underwater, so the state puts you to work

29
00:03:06,000 --> 00:03:12,000
[29] Not slavery, closer to conscripted labour

30
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:17,000
[30] Fed, housed, and written down by name

31
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:25,000
[31] Pyramid builders: job titles, ration lists, their own cemetery

32
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:31,000
[32] Bread, beer and a surprising amount of beef

33
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:37,000
[33] The Great Pyramid is already over a thousand years old

34
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:44,000
[34] As ancient to you as Charlemagne is to a modern viewer

35
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:51,000
[35] Flood height sets your taxes

36
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:58,000
[36] Too high, and your village is underwater

37
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:04,000
[37] Scribes measure your field and claim a share

38
00:04:04,000 --> 00:04:10,000
[38] Farmers dragged in front of the assessors

39
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:17,000
[39] Nobody painted it as a scandal. It was just Tuesday

40
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:24,000
[40] Builders, soldiers, priests, and scribes at the top

41
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:30,000
[41] A scribe lifted nothing. A scribe paid less

42
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:36,000
[42] Schoolboys handed a text about every other trade

43
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:44,000
[43] Every trade described as filthy: careers propaganda

44
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:50,000
[44] The enormous temples you were never allowed inside

45
00:04:50,000 --> 00:04:56,000
[45] The god's house, staffed by priests, closed to the public

46
00:04:56,000 --> 00:05:01,000
[46] Festivals: the god carried out in a boat

47
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:08,000
[47] Your actual religion happened at home

48
00:05:08,000 --> 00:05:14,000
[48] Bes and Tawaret on the shelf

49
00:05:14,000 --> 00:05:20,000
[49] A hippo goddess with a crocodile down her back

50
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:26,000
[50] The two most dangerous animals, reassigned as babysitters

51
00:05:26,000 --> 00:05:32,000
[51] Painted busts of dead relatives, and food left for them

52
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:39,000
[52] Natron paste, shaved heads, and a wig on top

53
00:05:39,000 --> 00:05:44,000
[53] Magic and medicine were the same job

54
00:05:44,000 --> 00:05:49,000
[54] Wounds packed with honey, which really is antibacterial

55
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:54,000
[55] They also prescribed spells

56
00:05:54,000 --> 00:06:00,000
[56] One to treat, one to fight, one not to treat at all

57
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:06,000
[57] A doctor writing down when to stop

58
00:06:06,000 --> 00:06:12,000
[58] Your real enemies are invisible

59
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:18,000
[59] Parasites in the river; mummies still test positive

60
00:06:18,000 --> 00:06:23,000
[60] Dust wrecked lungs and small cuts turned septic

61
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:28,000
[61] And then there is childbirth

62
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:33,000
[62] Giving birth crouching, supported by others

63
00:06:33,000 --> 00:06:39,000
[63] Painted birth bricks, one found at Abydos

64
00:06:39,000 --> 00:06:44,000
[64] Many women didn't survive, and neither did many children

65
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:49,000
[65] Marriage, though, is oddly familiar

66
00:06:49,000 --> 00:06:54,000
[66] No ceremony, no rite: you moved in together

67
00:06:54,000 --> 00:07:00,000
[67] Founding a household, and sorting out the property

68
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:06,000
[68] In legal documents, Egyptian women own property

69
00:07:06,000 --> 00:07:13,000
[69] They inherit, sell, sue, testify and start divorces

70
00:07:13,000 --> 00:07:18,000
[70] Under their own names, no male guardian signing

71
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:24,000
[71] Not standard elsewhere in the ancient world

72
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:30,000
[72] Legal rights are not equality: rations went to men

73
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:36,000
[73] She could take you to court, not take your job

74
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:41,000
[74] Education was for a minority

75
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:46,000
[75] Boys copied texts onto limestone flakes

76
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:51,000
[76] Their homework survives, with the teacher's corrections

77
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:58,000
[77] For the wealthy it was completely different, especially in death

78
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:04,000
[78] 70 days, natron, linen, a painted coffin

79
00:08:04,000 --> 00:08:09,000
[79] Or a stripped down version, or a shallow desert grave

80
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:14,000
[80] Where dry sand did the preserving for free

81
00:08:14,000 --> 00:08:19,000
[81] Which is how Egyptians learned the desert preserves bodies

82
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:25,000
[82] Body intact, name spoken aloud, food left at the grave

83
00:08:25,000 --> 00:08:31,000
[83] Or you stopped continuing

84
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:38,000
[84] Feeding the busts wasn't sentiment, it was maintenance

85
00:08:38,000 --> 00:08:42,000
[85] Cats killed the rats and snakes going after your grain

86
00:08:42,000 --> 00:08:47,000
[86] The famous mummified cats came much later

87
00:08:47,000 --> 00:08:53,000
[87] Bred and sold to pilgrims as offerings

88
00:08:53,000 --> 00:09:01,000
[88] Roughly 700 years after your morning. Egypt changed

89
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:06,000
[89] 14 hours later, you climb back up to the roof

90
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:12,000
[90] Bread, a family, a river, and a hippo goddess

91
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:17,000
[91] We remember Egypt through its funerals

92
00:09:17,000 --> 00:09:23,000
[92] The tombs are the exception

93
00:09:23,000 --> 00:09:27,000
[93] You are the rule: the boss, the money

94
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:32,000
[94] They wanted their kids to have it easier

95
00:09:32,000 --> 00:09:40,000
[95] You know what to do
